// 027 — Float precision issues — DESIGN NOTE
// In a language with IEEE-754 binary floats, 0.1 + 0.2 doesn't equal
// 0.3. It equals something like 0.30000000000000004. The fault isn't
// the language — it's that 0.1 and 0.2 are infinite repeating
// fractions in binary, the same way 1/3 is 0.333... in decimal.
//
// Typical fallout:
//   * Financial code storing dollars as f64 produces off-by-a-penny
//     balances under rounding.
//   * Equality comparisons (`a == b`) must be replaced by tolerance
//     checks (`abs(a - b) < epsilon`), introducing a new bug class.
//   * Accumulating large sums drifts — Kahan summation exists only to
//     paper over it.
//
// AiLang side-steps this for now by being Integer-first. When Float
// support lands, it will be IEEE-754 binary like everyone else — the
// precision issues come with the territory. For code that genuinely
// can't tolerate the drift, the answer is fixed-point (see 017) or
// arbitrary-precision rationals built on Integer.
SubRoutine.Main {
    // Demonstrated in integer "pennies" rather than 0.1+0.2, since we
    // don't have floats yet. 1 cent + 2 cents = exactly 3 cents.
    one_cent = 1
    two_cents = 2
    sum = Add(one_cent, two_cents)

    PrintMessage("1c + 2c = ")
    PrintNumber(sum)
    PrintMessage("c (exact, because integer)\n")
    PrintMessage("the 0.1+0.2 problem is binary-float-specific\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
